---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";
import { getProjects, getPosts } from "../lib/content";
import { timeline, timelineStateLabels } from "../data/timeline";
const projects = await getProjects();
const posts = await getPosts();
const current = timeline.filter(
  (entry) =>
    entry.state === "learning" ||
    entry.state === "in-progress" ||
    entry.state === "planned",
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Md. Afnan — web projects, structured information, and a developing practice in accessible interfaces."
  path="/about/"
>
  <div class="container">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="About / Md. Afnan"
      title="Making information easier to use."
      lede="I build for the web. My main project brings Bangladeshi legal information into a searchable, readable form."
    />
  </div>
  <div class="container personal-layout">
    <aside class="personal-margin" aria-label="Public work">
      <p class="folio-label">A working introduction</p>
      <p>{site.status}</p>
      <dl class="plain-facts">
        <div><dt>Projects</dt><dd>{projects.length} published</dd></div><div>
          <dt>Writing</dt><dd>
            {posts.length} blog {posts.length === 1 ? "post" : "posts"}
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <a class="text-link" href="/cv/"
        >View my CV <Icon name="arrow-up-right" size={15} /></a
      >
      <a class="text-link" href="/contact/"
        >Contact <Icon name="arrow-up-right" size={15} /></a
      >
    </aside>
    <div class="personal-reading">
      <section aria-labelledby="work-heading">
        <h2 id="work-heading">Starting with the information.</h2><p
          class="reading-lead"
        >
          A page can contain the right information and still be difficult to
          use. That gap is what interests me: how structure, language and an
          interface work together.
        </p><p>
          In <a href="/projects/nyayota/">Nyayota</a>, the subject is
          Bangladeshi law. Acts, chapters, sections and cross-references need to
          remain connected, whether someone is searching for a provision,
          comparing two texts or checking a citation. The project is educational
          and informational, not legal advice.
        </p><p>
          I use AI-assisted development. I maintain Nyayota as a personal
          project; the case study explains its structure, implementation and
          limits without claiming that I independently wrote every line of code.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="interests-heading">
        <h2 id="interests-heading">What holds my attention.</h2><dl
          class="interest-notes"
        >
          <div>
            <dt>Structured information</dt><dd>
              Documents become more useful when their parts can be searched,
              compared and cited. Nyayota is where this interest becomes a
              concrete project.
            </dd>
          </div><div>
            <dt>Interfaces for reading</dt><dd>
              Typography, spacing and hierarchy are practical decisions: they
              affect whether a long passage is comfortable to follow, especially
              on a phone.
            </dd>
          </div><div>
            <dt>Performance &amp; access</dt><dd>
              Static output, small payloads, keyboard navigation and reduced
              motion. A useful site should not depend on a powerful device.
            </dd>
          </div><div>
            <dt>Writing</dt><dd>
              Explanatory writing and longer work are part of the direction I
              want to explore. There are no published articles or writing
              entries on this site yet.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="current-heading">
        <h2 id="current-heading">Still in progress.</h2><p>
          This is a record of ongoing work and intentions, not a list of
          completed qualifications.
        </p><ol class="learning-ledger">
          {
            current.map((entry) => (
              <li>
                <>
                  <div class="ledger-meta">
                    <>
                      <span>{timelineStateLabels[entry.state]}</span>
                      <time datetime={entry.date}>{entry.date}</time>
                    </>
                  </div>
                  <h3>{entry.title}</h3>
                  <p>{entry.summary}</p>
                </>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol><a class="text-link" href="/vault/#progress"
          >See the learning timeline <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section class="container editorial-next">
    <p class="folio-label">Follow the work</p><h2>
      Start with the project.<br /><em>Then look at the evidence.</em>
    </h2><div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/projects/nyayota/"
        >Read Nyayota’s case study <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a
      ><a class="text-link" href="/vault/"
        >Public sources <Icon name="arrow-up-right" size={16} /></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
